A user unit named `duration` is the program's own, and is not Go's.

`duration` is a built-in because `lib/time/time.sngl` marks it
`#[marks.builtin("duration")]`, and a built-in is identified by its mark and
never by its name -- which is what makes every one of them shadowable by a user
declaration of the same name. Three places in codegen asked `u.Name ==
"duration"` instead, so the `unit duration { blip }` below was handed Go's
`time.Duration`: `3blip` compiled to `time.Duration(3) * time.Millisecond`,
`float(d)` divided by a millisecond, the `time` import was emitted for it, and
Kotlin held it as a `Long` rather than a `Double`. A `blip` is not a
millisecond and the program never said it was.

What it should be, and is here, is the ordinary single-base unit it looks like:
`type Duration float64` in Go and a plain `Double` in Kotlin, with `float(d)`
reading the magnitude straight out.

`testdata/unit_duration_cast.txtar` and
`cmd/sngl/testdata/unit_magnitude_compiles.txt` are the other side and pin the
stdlib `time.duration` still dividing at the cast.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `time\.Duration` -- a blip is not a nanosecond count; the program's own unit must not become Go's
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `"time"` -- the import came with the misidentification; a unit of the program's own names no Go package
deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `3L` -- Kotlin holds a duration as a Long of ms; this unit is a plain Double

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

unit duration {
    blip
}

component main ui.node {
    var d duration = 3blip
    var n = float(d)

    ui.text(value="n is {n}")
}

ui.window {
    main
}
